{
  "title": "Scapula Plating System",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/implants/423252/scapula-plating-system",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Oct 9 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Acumed Scapula Plating System offers indication-specific titanium plates that can address challenging fractures of the scapula, including the glenoid, medial and lateral border, acromion, and scapular spine",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The precontoured plates minimize the need for bending while acting as a template for optimal implant placement and guiding screw fixation to osseous regions.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Related Implants",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Acromion Plate**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Provides fixation for fractures along the acromion of the scapula spine",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Designed to closely match the patient's natural acromion curvatures, which may help in the reconstruction of even severely displaced acromion fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "An integral piece of the Scapula Plating System, and may be used individually or together with the Acumed Medial Border Plate, Lateral Border Plate, and Glenoid Plate",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Placement",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Once the plate’s ideal positioning has been selected, it is provisionally stabilized to the acromion and/or scapula spine with one of the three instruments provided:",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423252/images/ac005317-c225-451e-b927-1928422a5a5f_screenshot_2025-03-14_at_9.31.51_am.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Plate clamps",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Plate tacks",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Reduction forceps with serrated jaw",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Fit",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Designed in conjunction with William B. Geissler M.D.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "For traditional fractures where the fracture line is at the base of the acromion to the spine, the 6-hole plate is utilized",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "In instances where the fracture extends distally into the acromion, the longer 7-hole plate may be a better option",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The plate is placed on the superior surface to stabilize the fractures of the acromion and/or scapula spine",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "In the case of a non-union or malunion the curve of the plate can assist in the anatomic reduction of the acromion",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Indications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Acromion Fractures",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Os Acromiale",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Approaches",
      "table": null,
      "images": [
        {
          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423252/images/16780044-a15d-4374-8724-bd67103d7d3b_screenshot_2025-03-14_at_9.32.01_am.jpg",
          "caption": ""
        }
      ],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "A horizontal incision is made directly over the palpable spine and then curved anteriorly over the acromion",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Dissection is sharply carried down to the fascia where the skin flaps are elevated.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The origin of the trapezium and deltoid may then be subperiosteally dissected to expose the fracture of the base of the spine and/or acromion",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Nonlocking Screw Insertion",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The nonlocking screws may be placed unicortically or bicortically",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "If bicortical screws are used, it is important to not over-penetrate the distal cortex and potentially risk injury to the rotator cuff",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Locking Screw Insertion",
      "table": null,
      "images": [
        {
          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423252/images/832251a2-f98b-4c9a-9a77-1474790e1ced_screenshot_2025-03-14_at_9.31.37_am.jpg",
          "caption": ""
        }
      ],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Using the locking drill guide (MS-LDG35) and the 2.8 mm drill (MS-DC28), place the 3.5 mm locking screws (COL-3XX0) into the threaded holes so that there are at least three screws total (if possible) on each side of the fracture",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
